{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790300860,"nonce":"HP4kPvmK7RmyKHyMZARnQBCICq8=","title":"SSA: a variable written in an if statement inside a block expression used as a value keeps its old value","message":"Seen through the Lua frontend. A TypedExpression::Block whose statements include `if cond { x = v }` (x a local of the enclosing function) and which is used as a value (a let initializer, a call argument) loses the write: after the block, reads of x see the value from before it. The same block as an expression statement keeps the write.\n\nReproducer (zylua built at 7f05e95a, branch w2/L-debug-library, before its after-call read-back was rewritten as straight-line assignments):\n\n    local function setter(v) return debug.setlocal(2, 1, v) end\n    local function f()\n      local a = 1\n      local r = setter(2)\n      print(a, r)      -- lua5.4: 2 a; zylua: 1 a\n      setter(6)\n      print(a)         -- 6 on both\n    end\n    f()\n\nThe Lua lowering emitted, after the call, `let set = zl_dbg_unspill(...); if (set \u0026 1) != 0 { a = list[0] }` inside the block the call's value is held in. Identical with and without ZYLUA_LLVM=1. The Lua side now avoids control flow there, so this needs a reproducer built directly as typed AST to confirm it is the SSA builder (translate_expression's Block arm and the continuation_block hand-off) and not the frontend.","files":null}]}